{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,31]],"date-time":"2026-10-31T19:08:33Z","timestamp":1793473713626,"version":"4.5.0"},"reference-count":42,"publisher":"National Academy of Sciences","issue":"49","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2021,11,29]],"date-time":"2021-11-29T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1638144000000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by-nc-nd\/4.0\/"}],"funder":[{"DOI":"10.13039\/100000185","name":"DOD | Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","award":["HR0011-180200022"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["HR0011-180200022"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039\/100000185","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"publisher"}]},{"DOI":"10.13039\/100017023","name":"Paul G. Allen Frontiers Group","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","award":["12171"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["12171"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039\/100017023","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"crossref"}]}],"content-domain":{"domain":["www.pnas.org"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"short-container-title":["Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. U.S.A."],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2021,12,7]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Significance<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>Almost all organisms replicate by growing and then shedding offspring. Some molecules also replicate, but by moving rather than growing: They find and combine building blocks into self-copies. Here we show that clusters of cells, if freed from a developing organism, can similarly find and combine loose cells into clusters that look and move like they do, and that this ability does not have to be specifically evolved or introduced by genetic manipulation. Finally, we show that artificial intelligence can design clusters that replicate better, and perform useful work as they do so. This suggests that future technologies may, with little outside guidance, become more useful as they spread, and that life harbors surprising behaviors just below the surface, waiting to be uncovered.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1073\/pnas.2112672118","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2021,11,29]],"date-time":"2021-11-29T17:19:04Z","timestamp":1638206344000},"update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1073\/pnas.cm10313","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":122,"title":["Kinematic self-replication in reconfigurable organisms"],"prefix":"10.1073","volume":"118","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9207-1775","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sam","family":"Kriegman","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Allen Discovery Center, Tufts University, Medford, MA 02155;"},{"name":"Wyss Institute for Biologically Inspired Engineering, Harvard University, Boston, MA 02115;"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7231-0196","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Douglas","family":"Blackiston","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Allen Discovery Center, Tufts University, Medford, MA 02155;"},{"name":"Wyss Institute for Biologically Inspired Engineering, Harvard University, Boston, MA 02115;"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7292-8084","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Michael","family":"Levin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Allen Discovery Center, Tufts University, Medford, MA 02155;"},{"name":"Wyss Institute for Biologically Inspired Engineering, Harvard University, Boston, MA 02115;"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Josh","family":"Bongard","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Computer Science, University of Vermont, Burlington, VT 05405"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"341","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2021,11,29]]},"reference":[{"key":"e_1_3_4_1_2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1063\/1.5038337"},{"key":"e_1_3_4_2_2","doi-asserted-by